post("CT-01", "contrarian", "static",
     "Unpopular: your fitting cost is not a percentage.",
     "Every window quote in Britain prices fitting as a percentage of the units. Every single one.\n\nThe problem is that a fitted casement into a good square opening is 40 minutes. A heritage box sash into a 1970s retrofit with a buried lintel is eleven hours. A french door under a soffit you cannot reach is a day and a half and a prayer.\n\nIt is not a percentage. It is a programme. Price it as a programme and you will find out you have been giving away six percent of every job for a decade.",
     "Photorealistic wide shot of a tradesperson on a step ladder trying to reach the top of a window set high under a deep soffit in a UK house, one arm fully extended, another tradesperson watching and laughing, tool belt, comic and relatable",
     "photo-trades", "contrarian", cta="Fitting priced as a phase, not a percentage. fenora.pro",
     li_lead="Pricing fitting as a percentage is the single most expensive habit in UK window manufacturing. Here is the argument against it.")

post("CT-02", "contrarian", "static",
     "“Cheapest quote” is not the cheapest job.",
     "The cheapest quote is the job that is missing its most expensive part.\n\nThe scaffold is not in it.\nThe making good is not in it.\nThe second visit is not in it.\nThe skip is not in it.\nThe 1970s electrics behind the plaster are not in it.\n\nThey are all on page four. Page four is written after the customer has signed page one.",
     "Photorealistic overhead photograph of a multi-page window quote with the first page showing a happy low total and later pages containing dense small print exclusions, a highlighter parked on a line, dramatic side lighting, slightly sinister",
     "photo-real", "contrarian")

post("CT-03", "contrarian", "carousel",
     "Your CRM does not know what a window is",
     "Slide 1: It has a field called “Product”.\n\nSlide 2: You put “8 windows” in it.\n\nSlide 3: It cannot tell whether they are timber or uPVC, whether they are glazed, whether the survey has been done, whether the glass is ordered, or whether the money has arrived.\n\nSlide 4: It cannot tell you if the job is profitable. Not even roughly. Not even badly.\n\nSlide 5: It is not a CRM. It is a phone book with a deal stage.",
     "Photorealistic dark-mode software interface floating at an angle showing a generic CRM contact record with a single text field containing a window count, deliberately sparse and slightly sad, neutral background, product photography lighting",
     "screen-mock", "contrarian", cta="A CRM that knows what a window is. fenora.pro")

post("CT-04", "contrarian", "static",
     "Seven systems and one person keeping them in step",
     "Most window businesses do not have one system. They have seven, and one person in the middle, and that person is on annual leave in August.\n\nCRM. Spreadsheet. Survey app. Quoting software. WhatsApp. Calendar. Delivery app.\n\nThe whole business is load-bearing on one person remembering that all seven are correct today. That is not a process. That is a personality.",
     "Photorealistic photograph of a cluttered tradesperson's office desk with a laptop, a clipboard, a spiral-bound survey book, a wall calendar, a phone showing a chat, a delivery app on a tablet, highlighter pens, coffee, warm chaotic light, relatable",
     "photo-real", "contrarian", cta="Seven systems to one. fenora.pro")

post("CT-05", "contrarian", "static",
     "The remake is the most expensive thing in the business.",
     "A remake is not just timber and glass.\n\nA remake is a re-survey, a re-quote, a renegotiation, a new delivery, a second install, a second set of making good, a second customer phone call, a second apology and a customer who will now mention it to everyone they know.\n\nOne remake costs more than a year of software.\n\nOne remake almost always starts with two systems and one missed email.",
     "Photorealistic photograph of a stack of ruined window frame offcuts and broken glass in a workshop skip, a broken IGU with a cracked pane visible, a gloved hand hovering, harsh light, melancholy and forensic",
     "photo-real", "contrarian", li_lead="If you only measure one number in window manufacturing, measure the remake rate. Everything else is downstream of it.")

post("CT-06", "contrarian", "reel",
     "Stop calling it a survey. It is an investigation.",
     "A survey is not someone pointing a tape at a hole.\n\nA survey is: is this wall load-bearing. Where is the cavity. Why is there a pipe. Is the lintel original. Is that plaster load-bearing or is that 30mm of nothing. Is the reveal square. Is the cill going to be level. Is the render sound. Is the cavity wall insulated. Is the floor in the way. Is the socket in the wrong place.\n\nAnd it is all captured in a way the office can actually use, the workshop can actually trust, and the installer can actually see on the day.",
     "Photorealistic 9:16 shot of a surveyor in a UK room with a laser measure, a probe, a torch, a notebook and a phone, shot from a low dramatic angle with dust in a beam of light, serious and cinematic, documentary hero shot",
     "photo-trades", "contrarian", img="9:16", cta="The survey app that IS the quote. fenora.pro")

post("CT-07", "contrarian", "static",
     "“We’re just a family firm.” You are a software company too.",
     "Every person in this trade carries a small, bespoke, undocumented business logic in their head.\n\nRates. Supplier relationships. What a rough opening is worth. Which fitter to send. Which customer pays. Which job to do first. How much to apologise. When to walk away.\n\nIt is not written down. It is not tested. It is not backed up. It works right up until the day it is needed and cannot be found.\n\nThat is what software is: making the person in the head legible.",
     "Photorealistic contemplative photograph of an older tradesperson in a workshop, hand resting on a timber window frame, tools and dust in the air, a quiet thoughtful mood, single shaft of window light, documentary portrait",
     "photo-trades", "contrarian", cta="Write it down. Once. fenora.pro",
     li_lead="Most small businesses already run on software. It is just in someone's head, undocumented, untested, and gone the day they take a holiday.")

post("CT-08", "contrarian", "static",
     "The most expensive sentence in construction.",
     "“It’ll be fine.”\n\nSaid about a cill. Said about a foam bead. Said about a frame on a bad reveal. Said about a scaffold. Said about a load on a van.\n\nIt will probably be fine. It is also, in the rare occasion it is not, the most expensive sentence anyone in the room will ever utter, and there is never a second chance attached to it.",
     "Photorealistic close-up of two tradespeople in a doorway, one talking and gesturing, one listening with arms folded, a questionable window installation visible behind them, tense standoff, moody natural light, cinematic",
     "photo-real", "contrarian")

post("CT-09", "contrarian", "carousel",
     "We will not publish your testimonials.",
     "Slide 1: You are about to spend a year of your life on this.\n\nSlide 2: You will not put our logo on your van, or let us photograph your work, or write a paragraph for our website.\n\nSlide 3: And that is completely fine.\n\nSlide 4: So we will not publish numbers we cannot evidence either. No invented case studies. No “300% ROI”. No testimonials with suspiciously round figures.\n\nSlide 5: We publish four measures: time to quote, remakes, margin held, and re-entries. Real customers. Real figures. Agreed before publication.",
     "Photorealistic understated dark-mode web page floating at an angle showing a clean, sparse layout with four simple stat blocks and no photographs, restrained and honest, neutral background, minimal product photography",
     "screen-mock", "contrarian", cta="Proof, not adjectives. fenora.pro",
     li_lead="We do not publish customer testimonials. We publish four measurable outcomes instead, and only with the customer's agreement on the figures. Here is why.")

post("CT-10", "contrarian", "static",
     "Nobody wants another generic window advert.",
     "Nobody in this country has ever looked at a picture of a bright white bay window in an empty lounge and thought “I want one of those.”\n\nThey want their own house to not be cold in January. They want to stop hearing the road. They want the bathroom to not smell. They want to be able to open something without negotiating with a swollen frame.\n\nThe whole industry advertises the product and none of it advertises the outcome. Here is the outcome.",
     "Photorealistic warm photograph of a lived-in UK family kitchen in winter evening, condensation on the new window gone, a kettle steaming, warm lamp light, a family blurred in the background, genuinely cosy, not a showroom, documentary warmth",
     "photo-real", "contrarian", cta="Windows, properly specified. fenora.pro")

post("CT-11", "contrarian", "static",
     "The customer is not comparing your price. They are comparing your patience.",
     "Three companies quoted. All three about the same money.\n\nThe one who rang back on the Tuesday about the reveal, sent the photo of the lintel, warned them about the cavity, and told them honestly that the cheaper option was a risk — got the job.\n\nNobody chose the best window. They chose the person who had clearly already been to the house in their head.",
     "Photorealistic photograph of a tradesperson's desk mid-conversation, a phone showing an outgoing call, printed survey photos spread out, a window frame sample, warm lamp light, thoughtful and human, documentary",
     "photo-trades", "contrarian")

post("CT-12", "contrarian", "static",
     "Unpopular: nobody is coming to you for the glass.",
     "The public will buy a window off a price. The trade will not.\n\nA fitter does not care about your spacer bar. A fitter cares whether the frame is heavy, whether the packers are in the van, whether the person selling it picked up the phone, and whether the size on the drawing is the size that arrives.\n\nStop selling to the public. Serve the fitter properly and the public comes with them.",
     "Photorealistic portrait photograph of a UK window fitter in workwear standing confidently on a residential doorstep, holding a tablet, looking directly at camera, warm morning light, shallow depth of field, capable and human",
     "photo-trades", "contrarian", li_lead="This industry markets to homeowners. It should market to fitters. Every serious conversion in the trade happens because a fitter told a homeowner it was fine.")

post("CT-13", "contrarian", "static",
     "The re-entry tax.",
     "Count it. Pick one job from enquiry to final invoice.\n\nTyped into the CRM. Re-typed into the spreadsheet. Re-typed into the survey app. Re-typed into the quote. Re-typed into the cut list. Re-keyed into the delivery app. Re-keyed into the invoice.\n\nNow count how many of those seven versions still agree with each other. Pick a job where somebody corrected the width on the third one. There is your remake.",
     "Photorealistic overhead photograph of a single job sheet covered in six different handwritings and annotations in six different pens, some parts crossed out, arrows everywhere, a coffee ring, forensic and chaotic, overhead light",
     "photo-real", "contrarian", cta="One record. Zero re-entries. fenora.pro")

post("CT-14", "contrarian", "static",
     "“We’ll sort it out on site.”",
     "“We’ll sort it out on site” is how every job becomes a five-day job.\n\nIt is a phrase that contains no information. It has no owner, no cost, no time, no material and no definition of done.\n\nIt is also, genuinely, the most reassuring sentence in construction, which is probably why we all keep saying it.",
     "Photorealistic photograph of a workbench with a scribbled note, a tape measure, a badly written list, a hi-vis jacket, and a van door open in the background with low evening light, warm and vague, aspirational ambiguity",
     "photo-real", "contrarian")

post("CT-15", "contrarian", "carousel",
     "The three things a window quote should tell you and usually doesn’t",
     "Slide 1: WHERE THE WATER GOES. Rain, run, down, out. Cill fall, horn detail, the whole thing. If that is not on the drawing, it is not been thought about.\n\nSlide 2: HOW BIG THE GLASS IS. Not the window. The glass. Sightlines eat 28%.\n\nSlide 3: WHAT HAPPENS IF IT IS WRONG. The variation process. Who approves it, who pays for it, how long it takes. If the quote does not say, you are carrying that risk, alone, forever.",
     "Photorealistic technical photograph of a window cill detail drawing on paper beside a physical installed cill, rainwater droplets beading and running, a slope indicator, warm desk light, analytical and beautiful",
     "photo-real", "contrarian", cta="Quote the details. fenora.pro")

post("CT-16", "contrarian", "static",
     "Your business is not a window business. It is a logistics business.",
     "You sell frames, but what you actually do is move a fragile, awkward, made-to-measure object from a factory to a specific opening in a specific house on a specific day, through a specific gate, past a specific dog.\n\nThe window is the payload. The job is the route. Most of the failures happen on the road and in the reveal, not in the factory.",
     "Photorealistic photograph of a delivery van loaded with window frames reversing through narrow UK terraced streets, a colleague spotting from a pavement, tight clearance, a wheelie bin, late afternoon light, documentary and dynamic",
     "photo-trades", "contrarian", cta="Load, route, conditions, signature. fenora.pro")

post("CT-17", "contrarian", "static",
     "Most window businesses cannot answer one question.",
     "“What did that job actually make you?”\n\nNot the quote. The job. After the glass, the fittings, the second visit, the van hire and the three hours you spent on a variation nobody invoiced.\n\nMost businesses genuinely do not know. Not because they are not trading, but because the information needed to know lives in four places and none of them talk.",
     "Photorealistic close-up of a hand hovering over a calculator on an office desk beside a job file and a coffee cup, dim late afternoon light, a sense of not knowing, contemplative and relatable",
     "photo-real", "contrarian", cta="Margin held, not just margin quoted. fenora.pro",
     li_lead="If you cannot answer 'what did that job actually make me', you are running a hobby with a payroll attached.")

post("CT-18", "contrarian", "static",
     "Unpopular: your lead generation is fine.",
     "You do not have a marketing problem.\n\nYou have a follow-up problem. The last lead that quoted with you and did not buy — have you rung them? Have you asked what happened? Have you rung them again in March?\n\nIn this trade, the average job is won long after the quote goes out, usually by whoever is still being polite. Most businesses stop being polite after two contacts.\n\nDo the boring thing eight more times.",
     "Photorealistic photograph of a tradesperson in a van making a follow-up call, a handwritten list of customer names on the dashboard, coffee, late afternoon sun, purposeful and unglamorous, documentary",
     "photo-trades", "contrarian")


